HB_FEX_SMC_HOT_MAXINST leaves JIT engines' code pools uncapped

Highball's HB_FEX_SMC_HOT_MAXINST (64 in the Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024 recipe) caps a compile unit that starts
on a writable page a write fault invalidated in the last 5 s, and ends such units at calls: for a copy protection
that keeps rewriting its code, small units are cheaper to translate again. JavaScriptCore's JIT writes a page of its
code pool now and then too (a new function, an inline cache, a jump repatched), so under the recipe nearly all of its
code ran as small units that end at every call: in churn.js, 79,646 of 82,357 compiles were capped. Microsoft Flight
Simulator's Coherent GT gauges run the same engine.

A JIT engine's pool is a private executable reservation of 256 MB or more (JavaScriptCore reserves one of 1 GB, at
0x7fff0000 in both Bun and the game); a copy protection's code sits in images and hook code in small allocations.
Units that start in such a pool are no longer capped. The ARM64EC frontend finds pools with NtQueryVirtualMemory the
first time a capped unit would start in one, lists them in a lock-free table (writers under their own mutex, as this
runs while compiling) and drops one when its reservation is released (MEM_RELEASE; decommits keep it).
SQUARK_FEX_HOT_JITPOOLS=1 caps them again. Without HB_FEX_SMC_HOT_MAXINST nothing changes.

Bun 1.4.4 for Windows x64, the FBW A380X gauges in squark's Coherent stand-in, MSFS's FEX switches, alternated
against 0040: PFD (Index=2) per frame 18.3-18.6M -> 16.7-16.8M instructions, 3.9-4.3M -> 3.5-3.6M cycles (the same as
HB_FEX_SMC_HOT_MAXINST=0; SQUARK_FEX_HOT_JITPOOLS=1 gives 0040's); the 24 HTML gauges 4.31/4.31/4.38 -> 3.93/3.94/4.17
ms per frame, text hashes unchanged; avionics.js 0.22-0.26 -> 0.18-0.19 ms; churn.js 760-791 -> 693-746 ms, checksums
as the Mac native run. Unchanged: the A380X WebAssembly hashes, the TFDi MD-11 replay (39.05M instructions,
9.41-9.56M against 9.48-9.74M cycles per frame), FBW A380X 2.644M instructions and 481k/481k against 479k/483k cycles
per tick, Highball's smcmsfs/smcdata/smcretire (6.09-6.66/3.38-3.56/2.91-3.07 s against 6.37-6.90/3.60-3.76/2.90-3.28
s), squark's regression suite, leatest, fault-test, smc-subpage (no stale value) and JSC stress (low tier-up
thresholds, 0/40).
